# Optional Training Features Reference

## Training loop constraints (read first)

- Training is config-driven: `train.json` + `heuristic.json`. No code changes per run.
- Self-play loss not decreasing is normal (opponent also gets stronger).
- Latest model always moves forward, never rolls back. Best model tracked independently.
- Eval must use the same compiled code as selfplay.
- The entire selfplay pipeline runs in C++. Never write Python fallback.
- ONNX model is mandatory for selfplay/arena/eval. No uniform-policy fallback.

## IHeuristic (engine/core/heuristic.h)

```cpp
struct HeuristicResult {
  std::vector<ActionId> actions;
  std::vector<double> scores;
};

class IHeuristic {
  virtual std::string name() const = 0;
  virtual HeuristicResult pick(
      IGameState& masked_state,
      const IBeliefTracker* tracker,
      const IGameRules& rules,
      std::uint64_t rng_seed) const = 0;
};
```

Input: perspective-masked state (same surface as encoder). `tracker` may be
nullptr for fully-public games. `masked_state` is non-const because some
heuristics use do_action_deterministic + undo_action for shallow lookahead
on the clone.

### Three heuristic flavors

| Flavor         | Key              | Description                                    |
|----------------|------------------|------------------------------------------------|
| Pure rule       | `"type": "rule"` | Hand-written scoring, no search                |
| Hand-written PV + MCTS | `"type": "mcts_pv"` (no model_path) | PV evaluator written in C++, wrapped in MCTS |
| ONNX PV + MCTS | `"type": "mcts_pv"` (with model_path) | ONNX model as PV evaluator inside MCTS       |

### Three selectors (independent)

| Selector               | Config location                    | Purpose                         |
|------------------------|------------------------------------|---------------------------------|
| heuristic_guidance     | `train.json::heuristic_guidance`   | Training-time guidance schedule |
| eval_vs_heuristic      | `heuristic.json::selectors`        | Eval baseline opponent          |
| web                    | `heuristic.json::selectors`        | Web hint provider               |

Each selector references a name from the HeuristicRegistry pool. Improving the
guidance heuristic does not pollute the eval baseline.

Config consumption:

- `heuristic_guidance`: `training/pipeline.py` reads `name`, `initial_ratio`,
  `hold_steps`, `steps`, and `temperature`, then forwards the resolved ratio,
  temperature, and selector name into the C++ selfplay runner.
- `eval_vs_heuristic`: `training/pipeline.py` reads selector `name` and
  `temperature`, then passes both into the eval-vs-heuristic bindings.
- `web`: `platform/game_service/sessions.py` reads selector `name` and
  `temperature`, stores both on the session config, and web AI / hint paths pass
  both into `GameSession.get_heuristic_action`.

### HeuristicRegistry (engine/core/heuristic_registry.h)

Per-game named pool populated in `<game>_register.cpp`, exposed via
`GameBundle::heuristics`. Lookup by name throws on miss (no fallback).
`heuristic.json::named` lists entries; `test_heuristic_registry.py` validates.
For `mcts_pv` entries, `simulations`, `c_puct`, and optional `model_path` are
runtime construction inputs: the C++ registration reads them from
`heuristic.json` before constructing `MctsPVHeuristic`.

### Temperature

Selection rule (argmax-with-tie or softmax(T)) is applied by the caller, not by
the heuristic itself. Most heuristic episode/eval paths use
`runtime::sample_heuristic_index`; selfplay guidance applies the same rule
inline in `selfplay_runner.cpp` when it converts heuristic scores into training
targets. All three configured consumers (`heuristic_guidance`,
`eval_vs_heuristic`, `web`) must treat `temperature=0` as uniform sampling over
argmax ties, not "first max wins".

### MctsPVHeuristic Factory Methods

Two static constructors on `MctsPVHeuristic` (`heuristic/mcts_pv_heuristic.h`):

```cpp
// Hand-written PV evaluator wrapped in MCTS
static std::unique_ptr<IHeuristic> make_from_evaluator(
    std::string name,
    const IPolicyValueEvaluator* evaluator,  // borrowed, caller owns lifetime
    const IStateValueModel* value_model,
    int simulations, float c_puct);

// ONNX model as PV evaluator inside MCTS
static std::unique_ptr<IHeuristic> make_from_onnx(
    std::string name,
    const std::string& model_path,
    const IFeatureEncoder* encoder,
    const IStateValueModel* value_model,
    int simulations, float c_puct);
```

Both return `std::unique_ptr<IHeuristic>` ready for registration in the
heuristic pool.

### heuristic_pv_evaluators (GameBundle field)

```cpp
std::vector<std::unique_ptr<IPolicyValueEvaluator>> heuristic_pv_evaluators;
```

Owns hand-written PV evaluators that `MctsPVHeuristic` instances borrow-point
to. Registered on `GameBundle` during `<game>_register.cpp` setup. Lifetime
is tied to the bundle (process-wide singleton), so the borrowed pointers in
`make_from_evaluator` remain valid for the entire process.

### TailSolveTrigger

```cpp
using TailSolveTrigger = std::function<bool(const IGameState& state, int ply)>;
```

Game-supplied predicate deciding when tail solve should attempt. Must be
registered on `GameBundle` alongside `tail_solver`. Runtime callsites check it
at each decision ply before setting `NetMctsConfig::tail_solve_enabled`; if it
returns false, MCTS proceeds normally even when `tail_solve_enabled = true` in
the profile. `NetMcts` itself only sees the already-resolved config.

## ITailSolver (engine/search/tail_solver.h)

```cpp
class ITailSolver {
  virtual TailSolveResult solve(
      IGameState& state, const IGameRules& rules,
      const IStateValueModel& value_model,
      int perspective_player,
      const TailSolveConfig& config) const = 0;
};
```

Alpha-beta search on `do_action_deterministic + undo_action`. The implementation
lives in `engine/search/tail_solver.cpp`; production callers route through
`NetMctsConfig::tail_solver` (MCTS/selfplay/arena/web) or explicit pybind test /
debug helpers. All of them rely on the deterministic action path.

TailSolveConfig fields: `depth_limit`, `node_budget`, `time_limit_ms`,
`auxiliary_weight`, `margin_scorer`.

Safety: if a game has both belief_tracker and tail_solver, it MUST set
`stochastic_tail_solve_safe = true` (explicit opt-in). Otherwise
`GameRegistry::create_game` throws at runtime. The game must override
`do_action_deterministic` so alpha-beta never consumes hidden chance outcomes.

BUG-004: tail solver + hidden info without stochastic_tail_solve_safe poisons
training samples (proven-win strategy correct only for one realization).

### Tail-solve tuning

Tune in this order:

1. Pick `node_budget` first. When tail solve hits budget, its wall time should
   be roughly comparable to the MCTS call it may replace; otherwise the search
   mix becomes unbalanced.
2. Tune `depth_limit` next. The goal is that, when the trigger fires, tail solve
   usually completes and returns a classified result (win/loss/draw), even if it
   does not prove a win.
3. Tune `tail_solve_trigger` last using game knowledge. Fire it only in
   high-value, low-branching, near-resolution positions where alpha-beta has a
   good chance to finish; avoid spending the budget in broad middlegame states.

## TrainingActionFilter

```cpp
using TrainingActionFilter = std::function<std::vector<ActionId>(
    const IGameState& state, const IGameRules& rules,
    const std::vector<ActionId>& legal)>;
```

Prunes legal actions to focus exploration. Returns a subset of `legal`; if the
filter returns empty, `FilteredRulesWrapper` falls back to the original legal
set.

Primary use is selfplay training via `training_filter_*` schedule. It can also
be enabled explicitly in analysis / Web AI profiles with `ai_use_action_filter`;
do not assume public inference always uses the full legal action set.

## AuxiliaryScorer

```cpp
using AuxiliaryScorer = std::function<float(
    const IGameState& state, int player, int depth_remaining)>;
```

Tiebreak bonus for tail solver among proven wins. `depth_remaining` lets
game-side scorers fold "prefer faster wins". Recommended shape:
`depth_remaining + 0.1f * normalized_margin`, weight = 0.01.

## GameAdjudicator

```cpp
using GameAdjudicator = std::function<int(const IGameState& state)>;
```

Fallback judge for games that hit `max_game_plies` without natural terminal.
Returns winner player index, or -1 for draw / no adjudication. Selfplay calls it
after the episode loop when the state is still non-terminal, not every ply.

## EpisodeStatsExtractor

```cpp
using EpisodeStatsExtractor = std::function<
    std::map<std::string, double>(
        const IGameState& final_state,
        const IGameState& initial_state,
        const std::vector<SelfplaySampleView>& samples)>;
```

Post-episode statistics for logging/diagnostics. Receives both final and initial
state plus the full sample trace. `SelfplaySampleView` includes `ply`, `player`,
`action_id`, and a `features` pointer (borrowed, valid only inside callback).

### Custom stats in train.log

The map returned by the extractor is averaged over all episodes in the step and
logged to train.log as key=value pairs. Naming conventions:

- Use `_rate` suffix for rate-like metrics — formatted to 3 decimal places.
- Use plain names for count-like metrics — formatted to 1 decimal place.

Example (Coup): registers `opening_duke_rate`, `opening_duke_challenged_rate`,
`opening_duke_real_rate` — these appear in train.log as averaged per-step values
alongside the standard loss/win-rate columns.

## Opponent pool

Configured by `train.json::training.opponent_pool_enabled` and
`opponent_pool_self_ratio`. When enabled, each selfplay batch mixes mirror games
against the latest model with games against historical `model_step_*.onnx`
checkpoints from the current run's `models/` directory. The best model is
tracked separately for gating and is not the opponent pool source.

## Config defaults and validation

Training reads `train.json`, `heuristic.json`, and optional `belief.json`.
`mcts.json` and profile deltas are resolved through
`training.mcts_profile.resolve_profile`; unknown MCTS profile fields raise.

Important defaults / guards:

- `training.steps`, `episodes_per_step`, `batch_size`, `learning_rate`, and
  `runtime.*` have CLI-level defaults, but game configs should spell out real
  run recipes.
- `weight_decay` defaults to `1e-4`; `grad_clip_norm` defaults to `1.0`;
  `train_batches_per_step` defaults to `3`.
- `replay_buffer_size` defaults to `episodes_per_step * 50 * 20`.
- Missing `selfplay_concurrency` means legacy multi-process selfplay. If present
  with `enabled=false`, no other keys may appear in that block.
- `belief.json` is shape/loss only. PV-side schedule knobs are forbidden there;
  belief training mirrors PV batch size, LR schedule, batches/step, and weight
  decay.
- `heuristic.json::named.<mcts_pv>` is runtime-significant:
  `simulations`, `c_puct`, and optional `model_path` are read by C++ registration
  when constructing `MctsPVHeuristic`.

## Known issues

- BUG-004: tail solver + hidden info safety gate (see ITailSolver section above)
- Legacy heuristic_guidance keys (`heuristic_guidance_*`,
  `heuristic_temperature`) in `train.json::training` hard-fail via
  `find_train_config` ValueError. Use `train.json::heuristic_guidance` (top-level).
